    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d207">

                <pb facs="0176" n="170" sameAs="frus:frus1939v04/pg170"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102 Tientsin/229: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Consul General at
                        Tientsin</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caldwell</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-03-27T19:00:00-05:00">March 27, 1939—7 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>15. Your 43, March 23, 6 p.m. Bearing in mind the considerations outlined in the
                    first paragraph of the Department’s 110, December 15, 4 p.m.,<anchor n="54"
                        xml:id="d207-f1"/> you may notify the Japanese Consul General of the number
                    of Americans residing in Tientsin and of the number of passes that will be
                    needed.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Peiping and Chungking.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
